window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][127] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C08 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Google</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you improve the inbox of a widely used email app for people who receive more than 100 emails a day?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Lists features (smarter search, more labels, a dark mode) without saying who the user is or where the inbox fails them.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States what the product is for, chooses a segment and a step, names a specific problem, offers different types of move and recommends one.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats a wrong automatic decision as the main risk, protects people who rely on the current view, sizes the gain and sets a guardrail on missed important mail.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll assume the existing app on phones and the web, and a goal of making the inbox the place where heavy email users get their work done. I'll note that I know the product only from public information and use. <em>State.</em> Role: a utility that anchors the user to the company's wider account and services. Users: people with high volume (managers, sales, support, executives), moderate users, and casual users. Funnel: mail arrives, the person triages (decides what needs action), reads, acts, then files or deletes. Metrics I'd expect it to follow: daily use, time in the app, and search use. Constraints: years of mail in people's folders and labels, habits around a chronological view, and the same behavior across devices. <em>Area.</em> I choose people receiving 100 or more emails a day at the triage step. Their reach is smaller than the whole base, perhaps 5% of users (invented), but the pain is highest, the effort repeats every day and they influence which tool their teams use. I give up casual users, who have little to improve. <em>Problem.</em> A heavy user cannot tell quickly which messages need a reply from them and which only inform. The workarounds are the evidence: starring, moving mail into folders by hand, and rereading the inbox several times a day. I'd verify by watching three people triage and by looking at how many messages people open and then leave unanswered. <em>The four moves.</em> Fix: make the existing importance signals more accurate and more visible, since wrong ones teach users to ignore them. Add: a &quot;needs reply&quot; section built from signals such as being addressed directly, a question in the text and the person's past replies to that sender. Remove: take out manual steps in triage, such as having to label a thread to keep track of it. Reposition: treat the inbox as a task list. <em>Size.</em> If 5% of 500,000,000 users (invented) are heavy, that is 25,000,000 people. If triage takes 40 minutes a day and the section saves 10% of it, that is 4 minutes a day per person; with a 30% haircut for people who ignore the section, about 2.8 minutes, so about 70,000,000 minutes a day across the group, or about 1.2 million hours a day. The number is large because of scale, and I'd still check it with a test, since it assumes people trust the section. <em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend the Add, with the Fix, in the first version: a &quot;needs reply&quot; section above the normal inbox, never replacing it, with the reason shown (&quot;addressed to you, contains a question&quot;). It addresses the triage problem by giving up a more ambitious reposition. Messages are only highlighted, never hidden, because a wrong decision that buries an important email is the worst failure and the one that destroys trust. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of messages in the section that the user replies to within 24 hours. Input: the share of heavy users who open the section daily. Guardrail: the share of replied-to messages that the section had not flagged, which measures what it misses, and it should stay under 5% (invented). Counter metric: the share of flagged messages that the user dismisses without action, which measures noise. <em>Risks.</em> Legacy users who rely on the chronological view keep it as the default, with the section optional. Misjudged importance could favor some senders, so the signals are shown and explained, and the user can turn them off. I'd change my mind if watching users showed that triage was fast already and the cost was in writing replies, in which case the lever is drafting help and not sorting. <em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that improvement prompts at Google can concern its own products at large scale <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or product behavior is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c08-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C08-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C08 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Improve the order-tracking screen of a food-delivery app.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add a map and push notifications,&quot; with no reasoning about the problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies the customer's problem while waiting, chooses a fix over an addition where it is better, sizes it and gives a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sees that an honest estimate can hurt conversion at checkout, names the second-order effect and keeps a counter metric on it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The product is a marketplace and its role is revenue and retention: customers who get food when they expect it come back. The segment is customers who have ordered and are waiting. The step is from &quot;order placed&quot; to &quot;delivered,&quot; and the pain is uncertainty: they do not know when the food will arrive or why it is late. The evidence I'd look for is contacts to support asking &quot;where is my order?&quot;, which I'd assume at 6 per 100 orders (invented), and the cost of each contact, assumed at $3 (invented). <em>Moves.</em> Fix: make the estimated time accurate, and show a range with a reason when something changes (&quot;the restaurant is taking longer&quot;). Add: a live map of the courier. Remove: take away the minute-by-minute countdown that shows precision the app does not have; a countdown that is wrong damages trust more than a range. Reposition: none. I recommend the Fix with the Remove: honest, accurate estimates, which cost less than a map and address the cause of the contacts. <em>Size.</em> With 1,000,000 orders a month (invented), 6% contact support: 60,000. If the change cuts contacts by 25%, that is 15,000 fewer; with a 30% haircut, 10,500, or about $31,500 a month in support cost. The cost saving is modest, and the larger effect is likely on satisfaction and reordering, which I'd measure and not assume. <em>Second-order effect.</em> An honest range is longer than an optimistic number, and the estimate shown at checkout affects whether a customer orders at all. If I show &quot;35 to 45 minutes&quot; where the app said &quot;30,&quot; conversion could fall. So the checkout estimate and the tracking estimate must come from the same model, and I'd test checkout conversion as a guardrail. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: support contacts about delivery time per 100 orders. Input: the share of orders delivered within the shown range. Guardrail: conversion at checkout. Counter metric: the share of customers who cancel after seeing a longer estimate, which shows whether honesty is driving them away. I'd change my mind if the data showed that most contacts were about missing items rather than timing, in which case the lever is order accuracy.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c08-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C08-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C08 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Improve a notes app that syncs across phone, tablet and laptop.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add tags, a better editor and handwriting support,&quot; with no reasoning about the cross-device experience.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Focuses on the cross-device problem, names legacy users and consistency as constraints, and chooses a fix over a feature.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats trust as the product, chooses the metric that matches the failure (lost content, not engagement), and handles encryption, older versions and offline editing.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll assume the notes app that comes with the user's devices, and I know it only from public information and use. Role: a utility that makes each device more useful and keeps users within the same set of devices; its value grows with every device a person adds, so cross-device behavior is central. Users: heavy note-takers (students and professionals with thousands of notes), casual users (lists and reminders) and people who share notes. Funnel: capture, organize, find, revisit on another device, share. Constraints: years of notes and folder structures, consistent behavior across every device, offline editing, encrypted notes, and devices running older versions. <em>Area.</em> I choose heavy note-takers at the moment of opening a note on a second device. Their stakes are the highest because their notes are their work, and the step has a specific failure: a note edited on one device appears stale or differs on another, or two edits clash and one is lost. I give up casual users, who rarely feel it. I'd verify by looking at how often a note changes on two devices within a short time and what users do after a conflict. <em>Problem.</em> &quot;I edited a note on my phone, opened it on my laptop and saw the old version, or lost a paragraph.&quot; The workarounds are the evidence: copying text into a message to myself, saving a second copy, or emailing notes to myself. These show a loss of trust, which for a notes app is the heart of the product. <em>Moves.</em> Fix: show a clear sync state (&quot;up to date&quot; or &quot;syncing&quot;) and handle a conflict by keeping both versions and not by overwriting. Add: a version history, so any loss can be undone. Remove: nothing. Reposition: none. I recommend the Fix and a small Add: visible sync state, a keep-both rule for conflicts, and a short version history. <em>Constraint scan.</em> Legacy users: existing notes and folders must be untouched, and no change to how a note is stored should require action. Ecosystem: the behavior must work the same on every device, so I'd stage the rollout across all of them, and on devices on older versions the new behavior degrades gracefully, with no loss. Privacy: for encrypted notes the conflict is resolved on the devices, and the service never needs to read the note. <em>Size.</em> Assume 20,000,000 people use two or more devices (invented). If 4% hit a conflict or a stale view in a month, that is 800,000; if 10% of those lose content, 80,000. A keep-both rule that prevents 80% of losses prevents 64,000 losses a month. The count is small against the base, and the severity is high: one lost note can end a person's use of the app. So the size does not say &quot;skip it&quot;; it says to measure the right thing. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: lost-content incidents per 100,000 multi-device users per month, from reports and detected overwrites, with a target near zero. Input: the share of conflicts resolved by keeping both versions. Guardrails: sync delay at the 95th percentile, and storage used by duplicate versions. Counter metric: complaints about duplicate notes, since keep-both can clutter a library. I'd change my mind if the data showed that conflicts were rare and the common complaint was finding notes, in which case the area is search. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that improvement prompts at Apple concern its own devices and weigh consistency across them <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about how any existing notes product works.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c08-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C08-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C08 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Improve the walking experience in a maps app.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add augmented-reality directions and more landmarks.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments walkers, chooses a moment in the walking journey, names the problem, offers different moves and recommends one with a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the hardware (a watch, haptics) to keep the phone in a pocket, addresses distracted walking and battery, and protects users who rely on audio or who drive.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll assume the built-in maps app on phones and watches. Role: a utility that makes the devices more useful, with walking as one of several modes alongside driving and transit. Users: commuters walking to transit, visitors in an unfamiliar area, and errand walkers. I'll organize the walking journey as plan, start, move, arrive. | Step | Reach | Pain or loss | Headroom | Choice | |---|---|---|---|---| | Plan the route | High | Low | Low | Rejected | | Start walking: which way do I go? | High | Medium to high: the person does not know which direction to walk | High | Chosen | | Move: follow the turns | High | Medium | Medium | Second | | Arrive: find the right entrance | Medium | Medium | Medium | Rejected for now | <em>Problem.</em> At the start of a walk, the person standing on a street corner cannot tell which way to go. The map dot may not show the direction they face, so they walk a few steps, watch the dot, and correct. The workaround is the evidence (walking the wrong way for half a minute). Visitors in an unfamiliar area feel it most, since they have no sense of direction to lean on. <em>Moves.</em> Fix: improve the accuracy of the facing direction and give a plain first instruction (&quot;walk toward the park, on Oak Street&quot;). Add: turn cues on the watch, a tap for left and a different tap for right, so the person need not look at the phone, which also helps in the rain and in a crowd. Remove: reduce the map's detail while walking, since clutter makes the route harder to see. Reposition: none. I recommend the Fix with the watch cues. They use what the devices are good at (sensing direction, haptics on the wrist) and they address the start. <em>Size.</em> Assume 50,000,000 navigation sessions a week (invented), 8% on foot: 4,000,000. If 15% begin in the wrong direction, that is 600,000; the change cuts it by 40%: 240,000; with a 30% haircut, 168,000 avoided wrong starts. At 1.5 minutes saved each, that is 252,000 minutes, or about 4,200 hours a week. The gain is in time and confidence, and I'd say the value is the experience and not a number tied to revenue. <em>Constraints and risks.</em> Legacy users: drivers and transit riders must see no change. Safety: the point of the haptic cues is to reduce screen-watching while walking, and I'd test that screen-on time falls. Accessibility: people who use audio guidance should get the first instruction spoken as well. Battery: continuous direction sensing costs power, so it runs only while navigating. Privacy: the facing direction is computed on the device. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of walking routes whose first 30 seconds follow the route direction, inferred from location. Input: the share of walkers using watch cues. Guardrails: battery use per walking hour, and screen-on time while walking, which should fall. Counter metric: the share of users who turn the cues off, since cues that annoy will be disabled. I'd change my mind if the data showed that wrong starts were rare and the real loss was at the arrival (the wrong entrance), in which case I'd move to that step. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that improvement prompts at Apple concern its devices and the experience across them <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about how any existing maps product works.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c08-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C08-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C08 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Meta</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Many users of a social app say they spend more time on it than they want. How would you improve the product?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add a screen-time reminder,&quot; with no view of the conflict with the business or of who is affected.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the tension between time spent and user wellbeing, chooses a segment, offers different moves, and sets metrics that allow time spent to fall.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Agrees in advance how much engagement the company will trade, protects younger users with defaults, applies Goodhart's law, and says what would change the plan.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll start with the tension, since it shapes the answer. I'll assume the product earns revenue from advertising, so time spent matters commercially (an assumption I'd confirm), and that users are telling us the product sometimes works against their own goals. That is a goal conflict, and the interviewer will want to see how I handle it, not how I avoid it. <em>State.</em> Role: a core product and revenue engine. Users: people who say they spend more than they want, which I'd define by a short survey and by behavior (late-night sessions, long unplanned sessions), not only by volume; people who spend a lot and are content; and people who use it briefly. Funnel: open, scroll, interact, close. A feed that does not end has no natural stopping point. <em>Area.</em> I choose adult users who report that they spend more time than they want, at the moment of a long unplanned session. Their stated regret is a direct signal, their number is large, and the product's design contributes to the problem. I treat younger users separately with stricter defaults, because they are more vulnerable, and I'd involve safety and legal teams from the start; rules differ by country <code>[V: rules differ by country, for example India's DPDP Act treats anyone under 18 as a child]</code>. <em>Problem.</em> The feed has no natural end. People do not notice time passing, and the product offers no moment to decide to stop. Workarounds: deleting the app and reinstalling it, using the phone's own limits, and logging out. <em>Moves.</em> Fix: make the existing time tools easier to find and use. Add: a &quot;you're all caught up&quot; point after new content ends, and an optional reminder after a session length the person chooses. Remove: the next-item autoplay after the caught-up point. Reposition: from maximizing time to maximizing value, a change in what the product optimizes. Public accounts of one such shift exist: in early 2018 Facebook's chief executive said a change to the News Feed would reduce time spent in favor of more meaningful interactions, and the company reported a drop in time spent <code>Public</code> <code>[V: CS-C05-02, Facebook's January 2018 post and call]</code> (C05, CS-C05-02). I recommend the Add and the Fix first, with the Reposition as the longer question. <em>Metrics, and the trade.</em> Primary: the share of surveyed users in the segment who say they are satisfied with how they spend time on the product. That is a self-report and a proxy, and I'd pair it with behavior. Guardrails: long-run retention (a product that is healthier to use should keep people over years) and the reach of creators, who depend on attention. Counter metric: time spent and sessions per user, which I expect to fall, and I'd agree before launch how much of a fall is acceptable (say no more than 3% in the first quarter, invented), so that the team and leadership decide the trade in advance and not under pressure. Goodhart's law applies: if satisfaction became a target, it could be raised by changing how the survey is asked, so I'd keep the question fixed and audit it. <em>Risks.</em> Winners and losers: users in the segment gain; creators who depend on long sessions lose reach; advertisers see fewer impressions. Defaults have a large effect on what people end up with (<code>MM-defaults</code>, C07), so for younger users the reminders and limits are on by default. I'd reverse the break point if retention among the segment fell without a gain in reported satisfaction. I'd change my plan if the survey showed that the regret came mostly from one feature, and a targeted change would do more than a general break. <em>Company note.</em> Meta is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that improvement prompts at Meta can pair engagement questions with wellbeing and integrity tradeoffs <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Meta data or internal decision is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c08-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C08-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C08 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> An enterprise software product has a legacy report feature used by 4% of customers that consumes 20% of the team's maintenance effort. The team wants to remove it. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Remove it, since few customers use it,&quot; or &quot;keep it, since customers pay for it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks who the 4% are and what they pay, applies the fence test, compares options and sets a migration path.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies revenue at risk with and without a path, chooses a path that gives customers time and a replacement, defines how success and harm will be measured, and says when to stop.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat this as a removal decision with legacy users, and the number 4% is the start of the questions, not the answer. First the fence test (FW-C08-05): who is in the 4%, what do they pay, why do they use it, and why was it built? A small share of customers can be a large share of revenue, and enterprise customers often build routines around a report. <em>Assumptions.</em> 5,000 customers (invented), so 200 use the feature. They average $30,000 a year (invented), so $6,000,000 a year is at stake. The 20% of maintenance effort equals two engineer-years on a team of ten (invented), at $200,000 each fully loaded, so $400,000 a year. <em>Options.</em> | Option | Revenue at risk | Cost and effect | |---|---|---| | A. Remove it now | 15% churn among the 200 (invented): $900,000 | Frees $400,000 a year; breaks routines; damages trust | | B. Keep it and freeze it | Near zero | Saves part of the effort; the debt stays | | C. Replace it with a better report and migrate users over 12 months | 3% churn (invented): $180,000 | Frees $400,000 a year after the transition; costs migration work | | D. Keep it as it is | Zero | Keeps all the cost and the opportunity cost of two engineers | The arithmetic: 15% of $6,000,000 is $900,000, more than the $400,000 saved, so an abrupt removal loses money in the first year. With a path, 3% of $6,000,000 is $180,000, less than the saving, and the saving then continues. <em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend C: build the replacement, announce the change early, migrate the data and reports automatically, keep the old feature running for 12 months, and then remove it. It gives up the immediate saving. First I'd talk to a sample of the 200, especially the largest, to learn why they use it, and the replacement should meet that need and not just the feature's description. I'd check contracts and any commitment that names the feature. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of the 200 accounts migrated by the deadline. Guardrails: churn and contraction among the 200 compared with other accounts, and support tickets. Success: maintenance effort actually freed, and what it is used for; if it goes to the roadmap, I'd say what shipped. Counter metric: sentiment among affected accounts. <em>Risks and stopping rule.</em> The loudest accounts may be the strategic ones, so I'd treat each large account individually. I'd stop the removal, or extend the date, if migration is below a threshold set in advance (say 70% by month nine, invented) or if a strategic account says it will leave. I'd change the plan entirely if the interviews showed the feature does something nothing else does, in which case the product is missing a capability, and the question becomes how to build it well.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c08-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C08-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C08 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A mature productivity app has had flat weekly active users for six quarters, although the team has shipped many winning experiments. What would you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Run more experiments and add more features.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Questions why wins do not add up, looks at cohorts and segments, and considers larger moves.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Reconciles the sum of experiment lifts with the actual outcome, names the local-maximum risk, tests a new segment or job with a cheap bet, and sets expectations for the first quarter.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd start with the puzzle in the prompt: many winning experiments and a flat result. They cannot both be fully true, so something is wrong in how the wins are counted, or the wins are being offset. <em>Reconcile first.</em> Suppose the team's experiments claim an added 9% of weekly actives over six quarters (invented), and the actual change is zero. I'd list the usual reasons. Novelty: lifts fade after a few weeks, and a test measured over two weeks overstates them. Overlap: two wins can improve the same users and not add. Shifts: a win in one place can reduce use elsewhere (cannibalization within the product). Proxies: experiments may be won on a metric (clicks on a feature) that is a <a href=\"#gl-proxy-metric\">proxy metric</a> for value and is not the number that matters. Offsets: other things are pulling the number down, such as older cohorts decaying faster or new-user cohorts getting smaller. I'd check each with data, using cohorts (C05): are older cohorts retaining as before, and are newer cohorts arriving at the same rate and activating as well? The experiment mechanics are C14. <em>What the answer is likely to be.</em> If the flat number is a balance between decay and acquisition, the improvements are keeping the line level and the real question is growth. If new cohorts are healthy and older ones decaying, the problem is long-term retention. If new cohorts are small, the product may be saturating its market. Each leads to a different move. <em>Moves.</em> Fix: remove what is hurting retention for older cohorts (for example, complexity added over six quarters). Add: a capability for a job the product does not serve today. Remove: features that few use and that make the product harder to learn. Reposition: a different segment or job. The local-maximum risk is real: a team that optimizes steps one at a time will not find a better position, so alongside the steady work I'd run one or two larger, cheap bets, such as interviews and a prototype for an adjacent segment, with a stated criterion for success, and I'd protect them from the weekly metric. <em>Expectations.</em> I'd tell leadership that the first quarter should produce a diagnosis and a small number of bets, not growth, and I'd agree what we would look at: the reconciled lift, cohort curves, and the results of the bets. The primary metric stays weekly actives for the core segment, with retention by cohort as an input, and a guardrail on the health of the core experience (task completion, support load). A counter metric is complexity: the number of features a new user must learn. <em>What would change my mind.</em> If reconciliation showed that the experiment wins were real and the offset was a one-off event such as a change in how users are counted, the answer would be a data fix and not a product move. If the larger bets failed on their criterion, I'd concentrate the team on retention for older cohorts.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c09-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C09-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C09 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A messaging app wants to grow in a country where many users have phones with very little free storage. What do you change?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Make the app smaller,&quot; with no reasoning about who is affected or how storage is lost.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies how the storage problem shows up for users, chooses a segment, offers different kinds of move and recommends one with a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sees that received media, not the app, is the main consumer of storage, protects users who want to keep media, links storage to data cost, and states what would change the plan.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll state assumptions first, since I know this only in general terms: many phones have under about 1 GB of free storage, and these users are a sizable share of the target market. I'd verify that with device data on free storage at install and at uninstall. <em>Segment.</em> Users on phones with little free space. I choose them over users with ample storage because they are the ones losing the product. I give up users on high-end phones, who have no problem. <em>Problem.</em> A user's phone warns that storage is nearly full, and the easiest thing to delete is the largest app, or its media. The app is the first to go. Received photos and videos from large groups fill space quickly, and re-installing costs data. The workaround is the evidence: deleting the app and reinstalling later, or deleting media by hand. <em>The four moves.</em> Fix: make the app and its updates smaller (updates that send only what changed). Add: a storage manager that shows what uses space by chat and clears it in one tap. Remove: stop automatically downloading video over mobile data by default. Reposition: offer a &quot;lite&quot; version. I recommend the Add and the Remove together: a storage manager and a default that downloads media on request, which saves storage and data cost at once. The Fix is useful and smaller in effect, since the app is a minor share of what fills the phone. <em>Constraint scan.</em> Legacy users: people who like to keep every photo must be able to, so media they star is never cleared automatically, and nothing is deleted without a clear choice. Ecosystem: media removed from the phone should remain retrievable from the sender or the cloud where the product offers it, so nothing is lost for good. <em>Size.</em> Assume 100,000,000 users and 30% storage-constrained: 30,000,000. Monthly uninstall rate is 12% for them against 4% for others (invented). If the change cuts the excess in half (4 points), that is 1,200,000 fewer uninstalls a month; with a 30% haircut, 840,000. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: 30-day retention among storage-constrained users. Input: the share who open the storage manager. Guardrails: media delivery success and the rate of &quot;media not found&quot; reports. Counter metric: the rate at which users complain about lost media, which would show that the default is too aggressive. I'd change my mind if the uninstall data showed that few exits followed a storage warning, in which case the cause is elsewhere.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c09-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C09-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C09 · <strong>Competency:</strong> DI · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You have two weeks and a small budget to learn whether people in a market you have never visited would use a savings product. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Run an online survey and ask whether people would use it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Studies today's workarounds, uses local researchers, observes behavior, and sets a decision rule before starting.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Corrects for biases specific to the setting, tests with a commitment and not an opinion, spots the licensing issue in handling people's money, and says what would stop the project.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd use the discovery plan (FW-C09-04). The decision this informs is whether to build a savings product for this market and for whom. I'd write the rule before I start: proceed if there is a widespread workaround that costs people something real, and if enough people show a behavior and not only an opinion. <em>Week 1: workarounds, proxies and fieldwork.</em> I'd hire a local researcher, since they know the language and norms and will be trusted. I'd expect informal savings (groups that pool money, a shopkeeper or relative who holds cash), and I'd ask about the last time a person set money aside, where it went and what went wrong. I'd also look at proxies: how people top up their phones tells me how they handle small sums, and how they send money tells me about trust and distance. I'd visit three places (a market, a workplace, a neighborhood), speak to about 12 people, and watch what they do, not only what they say. <em>Week 2: a cheap behavioral test.</em> A concierge test: with a licensed partner, because holding other people's money without a license is not something I can do informally, a local agent collects small deposits from 20 people for four weeks and records them in a notebook, with a simple receipt in the local language. The signal is deposits made, not interest expressed. <em>Biases to correct.</em> Politeness: people may say they would use it to please a visitor, which is why I ask about behavior. Interviewer effects: a local researcher who is not visibly senior. Sampling: an online survey or a phone survey would miss people without a phone, so I'd reach people in person. Translation: I'd have the questions translated and translated back. <em>Decision rule.</em> Proceed if at least 12 of the 20 deposit in at least three of the four weeks (invented), and if interviews show that today's workaround costs people through fees, loss or risk. Stop or reshape if people save but prefer the informal route for reasons of trust that a product cannot change. What I would not conclude: that the market &quot;is not ready.&quot; If the test fails I'd find the reason.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c09-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C09-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C09 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Google</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design a feature for a video app for people whose connections are unreliable and whose data is expensive.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A lower-quality mode.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the binding constraint, ranks the pain points, offers different kinds of solution, and handles storage and data use.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Considers the effect on the people who make the videos, uses offline behavior as the primary design, protects users who want autoplay and quality, and sets guardrails on data use and storage.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll assume the existing app on mid-range phones, and a goal of more watching by people who now watch little or not at all because of cost and connection quality. Role: a core product that people choose for entertainment and learning. I know this only from public information. <em>Segment.</em> Viewers on a monthly data cap, on connections that drop, who commute or live where signal is poor. I give up viewers on home Wi-Fi, who have no issue. <em>Constraint stack.</em> Connectivity binds: cost and reliability. Devices follow: storage is limited, so downloads must be managed. Shared use and payment are secondary. <em>Pain points.</em> (1) A video buffers or fails mid-way and the data used is lost. (2) Autoplay and high default quality use the plan without the user choosing to. (3) The user cannot tell how much data a video will use. (4) Storage fills with downloads. I choose (1) to (3), which together are the data problem. <em>Solutions.</em> Assist: show the data used per hour and an estimate before playing. Automate: smart downloads of videos that the user is likely to watch, fetched on Wi-Fi overnight. Reframe: treat Wi-Fi as the place where the library is filled and mobile data as the exception. Remove: turn autoplay off on mobile data by default. Connect: let a friend share a downloaded video directly between phones. I recommend smart downloads with the data display and autoplay off on mobile data. They address the cause (data use at the wrong moment and place) and use an existing habit. <em>Constraint scan.</em> Legacy users: those who like autoplay keep a setting, and quality stays high on Wi-Fi. Ecosystem: downloaded videos must expire and respect rights, and people who make videos earn from views, so offline views have to be counted and credited. Storage: downloads are capped and cleared with a simple rule, such as removing the oldest watched ones first. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: watch time per month among capped users. Input: the share of watch time served from downloads. Guardrails: megabytes used per watched hour, and the share of downloads that expire unwatched, which measures waste. Counter metric: uninstalls linked to a full storage warning. I'd change my mind if capped users said their main problem was the cost of the plan itself, in which case a carrier partnership is the first move. <em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that prompts at Google often concern its products for users with limited connectivity <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or product behavior is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c09-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C09-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> product design · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C09 · <strong>Competency:</strong> PS · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Stripe</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A payments company wants to help small online merchants in a new country. How do you decide what to build first?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Support the local currency and the most popular cards.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Ranks payment methods by the share of payments they carry in the target segment, considers the licensing lead time, and builds in phases.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats licensing, verification and data storage as part of the product, finds the revenue model the market allows, and says how to learn quickly while the license is pending.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll assume a company with a payments platform elsewhere, and a goal of a first cohort of merchants taking payments reliably. Country details are assumptions: I'd verify them with public data on payment methods and with interviews. <em>Segment.</em> Small merchants who sell through social media and chat and take orders without a website, over established stores that already integrate gateways. They have the most pain and the least service. I give up the developers and stores where our existing product fits, and I'd serve them with the same platform later. <em>What the market uses.</em> Assume, for the target segment's sales by value (invented): bank transfers through a local QR system 55%, cash on delivery 30%, cards 15%. These numbers drive the ranking. <em>Options.</em> | Option | Share of target sales covered | Time to market | Risk | |---|---|---|---| | A. Cards first (our existing capability) | 15% | Short: about two months | Low, and a small market | | B. The local bank-transfer method | 55% | Longer: about six months, with licensing | Medium: regulation and a new integration | | C. Cash-on-delivery support and reconciliation | 30% | Medium | Medium: the cash is collected by a third party | <em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend B as the main build, since it carries the most sales, with the licensing and verification work started in week one because it has the longest lead time. In parallel I'd launch A to a small group of merchants who also sell abroad, to learn about onboarding, settlement and support, which would otherwise wait six months. C comes next, built as a reconciliation tool that matches courier-collected cash to orders. This gives up an early broad launch. <em>Beyond methods.</em> Merchant verification in local terms (a tiered process with lower limits at first, so honest sellers without formal papers are not excluded), data storage in the country if the rules require it, settlement timing in local business days, and a dashboard in the local language. Revenue: I'd check whether the local method carries a merchant fee by policy; if it does not, I'd price the software (reconciliation, invoices, reports) and earn on the minority of payments that allow a fee <code>[V: merchant-fee FAQs of 15 September 2026: a fee applies from 15 October 2026 to merchant payments above 2,000 rupees]</code>. <em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of target merchants able to accept their most-used method, and the share of payment attempts that succeed, by method. Input: time from sign-up to first payment. Guardrails: fraud and dispute rates, and settlement delay. Counter metric: the share of merchants who keep using their old method in parallel after three months, which shows a gap. I'd change my mind if interviews showed that cash on delivery was the dominant pain, in which case C would move first. <em>Company note.</em> Stripe is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Stripe publishes documentation on local payment methods and <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that prompts ask how to prioritize payment methods and compliance in a new country <code>[V: Stripe docs, Supported payment methods; the prompt descriptions remain candidate-reported]</code>. No Stripe data or roadmap is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c09-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C09-05</h3>\n";
